  "inline_comments": [],
  "issue_comments": [
    {
      "body": "I believe this fixes a real, silently-wrong-output bug — thanks for picking it up. \r\nA couple of notes that may help reviewers weigh correctness, especially on AMD backends:\r\n\r\nThe elif placement is load-bearing for the ROCm/FNUZ path — worth a comment so it isn't \"simplified\" away. On FNUZ platforms, k_scale has already been doubled a few lines up (if current_platform.is_fp8_fnuz(): k_scale *= 2). By applying the fallback in a new elif fallback_q_scale is not None: branch that sits before else, you correctly avoid re-applying the ×2. If a future refactor ever moved this into the if layer.q_scale > 0.0: branch (which does q_scale *= 2), FNUZ would double-count and halve-then-mis-scale the query. Suggestion: A one-line comment noting \"fallback is pre-adjusted for FNUZ; do not route through the *= 2 branch\" would help protect that.\r\n\r\nThis affects both NVIDIA and AMD backends. _q_scale is consumed whenever the backend sets supports_quant_query_input=True. On ROCm that's the AITER unified-attention backend (rocm_aiter_unified_attn.py) and the AITER MLA path, both of which quantize Q with _q_scale. So FP8-KV-cache models on those backends currently get q_scale=1.0 → E4M3 saturation above 448 → degraded attention. (The ROCm Triton backend gates this to current_platform.is_cuda(), so it's unaffected.) Might be worth widening the PR description beyond \"flash-attn or flashinfer\" so AMD backend users know the benefit for them.\r\n\r\nA small regression test would lock this in. The fallback path is otherwise uncovered. A CPU-only unit test that drives process_weights_after_loading with k/v scales present and q_scale omitted, asserting _q_scale resolves to k_scale (×2 when current_platform.is_fp8_fnuz()), fails on the old code and passes on this fix — happy to share a snippet if useful.",
      "created_at": "2026-09-27T04:16:34Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/51534#issuecomment-5852555825",
      "id": 5852555825,
